# test/mutants - the corpus: each record breaks ONE guard on purpose and names
# the test that must notice.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A HABIT. Every guard in this package was confirmed by hand
# at some point, and the discipline failed repeatedly IN MY OWN HANDS rather
# than in the code. On 2026-09-20 alone, five distinct ways a by-hand mutation
# silently did nothing:
#
#   an anchor copied out of output that had been indented for display;
#   an anchor containing a line-continuation backslash, which never matched;
#   replacing the FIRST of two identical lines, where the other one was the
#     guard the test covered;
#   mutating to something accidentally FALSE ([ -f /dev/null ] is false, so a
#     guard fired always instead of never and a different assertion died);
#   restoring one of two files a mutation touched, so it leaked into the
#     next case.
#
# Each of those produced a "passing" test that proved nothing. This file turns
# confirming a guard from something to remember into something that fails.
#
# FORMAT, one record per block, blank line between:
#
#   = <name>
#   f <file, repo-relative>
#   t <test names, space separated, without the .t>
#   - <the exact line to remove>
#   + <the line to put in its place; omit the line entirely to just delete>
#   m <optional: a fragment the failure MUST mention>
#
# Matching is EXACT, whole-line, and the target must be UNIQUE in the file: no
# regex, no delimiters, nothing to clash with the `||`, `*`, `$` and trailing
# backslashes these lines are full of. An absent line is a hard error, and so is
# an ambiguous one.
#
# `m` is what makes `killed` mean killed BY THE INTENDED ASSERTION. Without it,
# a mutation that happens to break an earlier, unrelated check counts as
# coverage. That is not hypothetical; see the fourth bullet above.
#
# GENERATED CAREFULLY, NOT TYPED. The lines below were located by substring and
# asserted unique, because hand-copying indentation is the first failure listed.


